ga5x(){return"Modified Julian Date 2000 = JD \u2013 2,451,544.5 or MJD \u2013 51,544 (2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC). Used in geodesy, geophysics, and spaceflight to simplify calculations with smaller numbers by shifting the reference to the beginning of the year 2000."},
ga5y(){return"New Earth Time (or NET), also called degree time, splits a day into 360 NET degrees, where each NET degree has 60 NET minutes and each NET minute 60 NET seconds. UTC is the only time zone."},
